::GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF, which vertex corresponds to B? {
=A. E#B and E are in the second position, so they correspond.
~B. D#D corresponds to A, not B.
~C. F#F corresponds to C, not B.
~D. A#A is in the first triangle and corresponds to D.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle PQR &cong; triangle XYZ, which side corresponds to QR? {
~A. XY#XY corresponds to PQ.
=B. YZ#QR uses the second and third vertices, so it corresponds to YZ.
~C. XZ#XZ corresponds to PR.
~D. PQ#PQ is in the first triangle, not the corresponding side in the second.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle LMN &cong; triangle RST, which angle corresponds to &angle;N? {
~A. &angle;R#R corresponds to L.
~B. &angle;S#S corresponds to M.
=C. &angle;T#N and T are in the third position, so they correspond.
~D. &angle;L#L is a vertex in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which congruence statement correctly matches A to D, B to E, and C to F? {
~A. triangle ABC &cong; triangle DFE#This matches B to F and C to E.
~B. triangle ABC &cong; triangle EFD#This matches A to E, not D.
~C. triangle ABC &cong; triangle FED#This reverses several corresponding vertices.
=D. triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF#The vertices are listed in matching order: A-D, B-E, C-F.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>What information is needed before concluding two figures are congruent? {
=A. Evidence that corresponding sizes and shapes match.#Congruence requires same size and shape supported by evidence.
~B. They are drawn near each other.#Location on the page does not prove congruence.
~C. They have the same color.#Color is not a geometric property for congruence.
~D. They look similar at a glance.#Appearance alone is not enough evidence.
}

